Context for 422a31d (T031): MVP_SPEC.md section 12 requires one total deadline that covers queue time, attempts, and backoff, with cancellation supported, and section 5 makes the wrapper own both while Rust validates the terminal transition. The design choice: the clock guards the deadline, not the timer delivery. The scheduler arms one wake-up through one injectable setTimer operation (the default computes the Node timer delay from the injected clock) but also re-reads now() before each settle and each drain, so a delayed or missing wake-up cannot admit one late result or start one late attempt; the test with one noop timer pins this. Cancellation enters as one caller AbortSignal option and propagates by aborting the internal controller with the caller's reason, which every attempt context already carries; a pre-aborted signal cancels before admission so every check records cancelled_before_start, which is how the cancel-before-start trace replays. Late results are dropped, not stated: the core would refuse them with late_result_rejected, so the scheduler drops the resolution, emits the same-code event for observers, and the frozen report never reaches the boundary. Every terminal path disarms the wake-up and removes the caller listener so no adapter or signal keeps one reference. Backoff between attempts stays unimplemented here; the deadline already covers its future duration because it is measured from run start. Four traces stay boundary-level rows of the native suite: duplicate-result and late-result-after-completion need one second resolution of one settled execution, which no promise can deliver, and deadline-keeps-completed and cancel-mid-run state one check_started order that disagrees with the FIFO drain this scheduler implements.
